  "title": "A homomorphism problem for flows",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "markdown-latex",
    "text": "Conjecture Let $M,M'$ be abelian groups and let $B \\subseteq M$ and $B' \\subseteq M'$ satisfy $B=-B$ and $B' = -B'$. If there is a homomorphism from $Cayley(M,B)$ to $Cayley(M',B')$, then every graph with a B-flow has a B'-flow.",
